{
  "name": "Making Your Garage Entry Accessible: What to Measure First",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Making your garage entry truly accessible means paying close attention to some key measurements right from the start. It’s not just about getting a ramp in place; it's about creating a safe and easy path for anyone using a wheelchair, scooter, or just needing easier access. The very first step is to measure the level changes. How much of a height difference is there from the garage floor to your home’s interior? This will directly impact the ramp slope you need. Next, you'll want to measure the clear width of your door opening. Will a wheelchair or scooter fit through comfortably without hitting the frame? People often ask about this, and it’s critical to ensure enough space for safe passage. Then, consider the landing space. You need adequate flat, clear space on both sides of the door, especially if a ramp is involved, so someone can maneuver their mobility device. Don't forget the door threshold height—even small bumps can be major obstacles. Finally, check your door hardware and ensure it's easy to operate from a seated position, with appropriate reach ranges. Many folks underestimate ramp sizing or ignore threshold heights, which are frequent mistakes. Focus on ramp slope, clear width, landing size, and door force. When you have significant level changes, or if the ramp and landing geometry feels tight, bringing in a qualified professional can really make a difference.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
